+    *** `MIEN' IS THE VENDOR'S OWN NAME AND IS NOT A TYPO FOR THIS HEADER'S `GENCTL_IEN'.
+    A panel seat read it as one on 2026-09-17 and it was refuted from the manual. ***
+    VMESBCP2's GENCTL NAME ROW reads `C040 MIEN FAST' against bits `10 9 8' of the word view,
+    so bit 1 of the byte at $FFF42002 is MIEN -- Master Interrupt Enable.  Calibrated against
+    two values this tree already pins: PCC2_C040 0x04 is byte bit 2 = word bit 10, and
+    PCC2_FAST 0x01 is byte bit 0 = word bit 8.  Both agree, so THE NAME ROW is aligned.
+    So the departure runs the OTHER way: PCC2_GENCTL_IEN (0x02, above) gives a NON-VENDOR name
+    to the bit Motorola calls MIEN, and PCC2_MIEN (0x02) names the same bit correctly -- two
+    spellings of one bit, which is what made the seat's reading plausible.  DO NOT "fix" the
+    sentence above to say IEN.
+
+    *** AND THE REASSURANCE ABOVE IS SCOPED TO THE NAME ROW: THE MANUAL'S **BODY** ON THIS PAGE
+    IS STAGGERED, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS NOTE SAID "the extraction's stagger does not
+    reach it" WITHOUT THAT QUALIFIER.  Found by the Opus 5 seat, 2026-09-17. ***
+    In vmesbcp2.txt the label `FAST' heads a BBRAM description; the label `MIEN' heads text
+    that begins "the FAST bit should be cleared..."; and the real Master-Interrupt-Enable
+    prose further down carries NO LABEL AT ALL.  **A round citing the MIEN label there gets
+    FAST and MIEN swapped.**  This is the carrier's Table-19.4 stagger class one layer down,
+    and its own refinement governs: calibrate on a row BELOW the value you cite, or on two
+    STRADDLING it -- a single calibration point is valid only for a UNIFORM stagger, and the
+    name row being clean does not make the body clean.  */
 #define PCC2_GENCTL_C040	0x03	/*  WRONG -- see above; use PCC2_C040 (0x04)  */
 
